    "id": "physics-1788729141049-87-314",
    "questionText": "In the diagram shown, If the south-poles of two magnets stroke a steel bar, the polarities at T and V will respectively be (JAMB 2018)",
    "options": [
      "north and south",
      "south and south",
      "north and north",
      "south and north"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "explanation": "Understanding magnetic induction by stroking:\nWhen a steel bar is magnetized by stroking with a magnet:\n\nThe pole that is stroked attracts the stroking pole\nTherefore, it becomes the opposite polarity\n\nFrom the diagram:\n\nTwo magnets with south poles (S) stroke the steel bar\nLeft magnet strokes from center (U) toward left end (T)\nRight magnet strokes from center (U) toward right end (V)\n\nApplying the rule:\n\nAt T: Stroked by south pole → becomes north pole\nAt V: Stroked by south pole → becomes north pole\nThe center (U) becomes a south pole (or weak point)\n\nResult:\nBoth ends (T and V) become north poles because both were stroked by south poles."
