    "id": "physics-1788729141053-518-1096",
    "questionText": "The equation PᵃVᵇTᶜ = constant reduces to Charles' Law if (JAMB 2014)",
    "options": [
      "a=1, b=1, c=0",
      "a=1, b=0, c=-1",
      "a=0, b=1, c=1",
      "a=0, b=1, c=-1"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "explanation": "Given information:\nCharles' Law states that at constant pressure, volume is directly proportional to absolute temperature: V ∝ T, or V/T = constant. This implies P is constant.\n\nSolution:\n\nStep 1: Interpret the given equation\nPᵃ Vᵇ Tᶜ = constant. For this to represent Charles' Law, pressure P must be constant. That means the factor Pᵃ must not vary, so its exponent a must be 0 (since P⁰ = 1).\n\nStep 2: Apply Charles' Law condition\nWith a = 0, the equation becomes Vᵇ Tᶜ = constant. Charles' Law requires V ∝ T, i.e., V¹ T⁻¹ = constant. Therefore, b = 1 and c = –1."
