    "id": "physics-1788729141056-697-378",
    "questionText": "An a.c of 1A at a frequency of 800 cycles per second flows through a coil, the inductance of which is 2.5mH and the resistance of which is 5Ω. What is the power absorbed in the Coil?\n (JAMB 2019)",
    "options": [
      "5W",
      "6W",
      "7W",
      "8W"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "Given information:\n\nCurrent (I) = 1A (r.m.s value)\nFrequency (f) = 800 Hz\nInductance (L) = 2.5mH = 2.5 × 10⁻³ H\nResistance (R) = 5Ω\nPower absorbed = ?\n\nKey principle:\nIn an AC circuit with resistance and inductance:\n\nPower is dissipated only in the resistance\nInductance stores and returns energy (no net power dissipation)\n\nFormula for power absorbed:\nP = I²R\nNote: The inductance affects impedance and phase angle but doesn't dissipate power.\nCalculating power:\nP = I²R\nP = (1)² × 5\nP = 1 × 5\nP = 5W"
